   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "Defining and reliably measuring 'shot mix' for short lineup intervals can be unstable. The granularity of shot locations and the minimum possessions required for a stable estimate will impact the validity of the measure."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "Given the potential subtlety of lineup effects on shot mix and the sparsity of data for many specific lineups, there is a risk of insufficient statistical power to detect true effects, leading to untestable or null results, as observed in previous research on offensive changes."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.25,
  "rejected": 0.3,
  "untestable": 0.25,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of a discrete change in the offensive five-player lineup on the team's distribution of shot attempts by location (e.g., restricted area, paint non-RA, mid-range, 3-point), controlling for game context, opponent, and individual player tendencies.",
  "unit": "Offensive possession. Data will be aggregated at the offensive lineup interval level.",
  "sample": "All offensive possessions from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, and 2024-25 regular seasons and playoffs. The analysis will focus on transitions between different offensive five-player lineups. A minimum threshold for possessions per lineup interval (e.g., 50 or 100 possessions) will be applied to ensure reliable shot mix estimation, potentially using a hierarchical model to pool information for sparse lineups.",
  "power": "A power analysis will be conducted to determine the minimum detectable effect size for changes in shot mix categories, considering the observed variance in shot mix within lineup intervals. The analysis will acknowledge the challenge of `EXPOSURE_SPARSITY`. Techniques like shrinkage estimation or empirical Bayes for lineup-specific effects will be considered to mitigate low power for rare lineups.",
  "controls": "To address confounding (e.g., 'shooter-level confound'), a panel data approach with fixed effects will be employed. This will include team-game fixed effects to control for opponent, game state, and overall team strategy, as well as player-level fixed effects for the 10 players on the court (5 offensive, 5 defensive) to account for individual player tendencies and skill levels. Additionally, a difference-in-differences approach comparing shot mix before and after a lineup change, within the same game context, will be considered.",
